   4: //! Named-pipe security descriptor for `ai-brainsd`.
   5: //!
   6: //! # Design (T144 + T184 F-1)
   7: //!
   8: //! The daemon may run as **LocalSystem** (Session 0 Windows service). CLI clients
   9: //! run in **Session 1+** as interactive users and must open the pipe. Owner-only
  10: //! (`OW`) is insufficient when SYSTEM creates the pipe.
  11: //!
  12: //! SDDL grants:
  13: //! - **SY** (Local System) - service host
  14: //! - **BA** (Built-in Administrators) - elevated operators
  15: //! - **IU** (Interactive) - interactive logon sessions (Session 1+)
  16: //!
  17: //! This replaces the prior **WD** (Everyone / World) grant, which contradicted
  18: //! T144 non-goals (multi-user World was listed as a security risk) and
  19: //! OPERATIONS prose ("interactive user").
  20: //!
  21: //! **Residual (R-MULTI / R-PIPE-IU):** On a multi-user machine, *any* interactive
  22: //! logon can open the pipe. Pipe messages still have no bearer (contrast HTTP).
  23: //! Single-owner desktops are the primary model; multi-user hosts accept residual
  24: //! risk documented in SECURITY-LIMITS.
  25: 
  26: use std::io;
  27: 
  28: use windows::{
  29:     Win32::Security::{
  30:         Authorization::ConvertStringSecurityDescriptorToSecurityDescriptorA, PSECURITY_DESCRIPTOR,
  31:         SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES,
  32:     },
  33:     core::PCSTR,
  34: };
  35: 
  36: /// SYSTEM + Administrators + Interactive (not World/Everyone).
  37: /// See module docs and T184 finding F-1.
  38: const SDDL: &str = "D:(A;;GA;;;SY)(A;;GA;;;BA)(A;;GA;;;IU)";
